llm_extracted_info.methodology:
The core methodology involves finetuning a denoising model (teacher model g
) into a new generator model (g
) designed for fewer sampling steps. The objective is to ensure that the conditional expectation of clean data is identical between the data distribution q and the sampling distribution g
 of the distilled model (Eg[x̃|zs] = Eq[x|zs]). This is achieved by minimizing the L2-distance between these moments. Two main algorithmic variations are proposed: 1. Alternating Optimization (Algorithm 2): This approach approximates Eg[x̃|zs] with an auxiliary denoising model (g
), which is trained using a standard diffusion loss on samples from g
 and regularized to stay close to the teacher model g
. The generator g
 and auxiliary model g
 are updated in alternating steps. Training involves randomly sampled time deltas for stability. 2. Parameter-space Moment Matching (Algorithm 3): This variant uses an instantaneous auxiliary model, where its parameters are determined by a single infinitesimal gradient descent step. This leads to a loss function (Linstant(n)) that can be interpreted as matching the expected teacher gradients in parameter space. It requires sampling two independent minibatches per parameter update. The distillation process uses standard ancestral sampling, adapted for fewer steps, and specific hyperparameters for noise schedules and loss weighting based on EDM, with an Adam optimizer and gradient clipping.

